        "title": "Repository Index and Chart Discovery",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 8, section 3: Repository Index and Chart Discovery. Explain the repository index.yaml file, chart entries, version metadata, package locations, and how Helm uses index information to discover available chart versions. Describe the importance of valid and accessible repository metadata.\n\nTechnical workflow and implementation: Start by inspecting the chart directory, the installed Helm version, and the relevant chart metadata. Identify the input files, repository configuration, dependency declarations, and values that control the operation. Use the appropriate Helm commands to validate the configuration and inspect the result. For chart authoring tasks, run helm lint and helm template with the intended values files. For repository or dependency tasks, verify the source, resolved version, downloaded artifact, and package contents. Do not assume that a successful command proves the resulting chart is suitable for production.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Create a small test chart or use an approved sample chart in a non-production workspace. Apply the configuration described in this section, execute the relevant Helm workflow, and capture the command output. Inspect the resulting metadata, dependency package, or rendered Kubernetes manifests. Repeat the procedure from a clean workspace or CI runner to confirm that the result does not depend on undocumented local settings. Record the exact chart version and configuration used during the exercise.\n\nProduction engineering considerations: Explain the repository index.yaml file, chart entries, version metadata, package locations, and how Helm uses index information to discover available chart versions. Describe the importance of valid and accessible repository metadata. Apply peer review to changes affecting chart sources, dependency versions, repository configuration, credentials, and release values. Keep secrets out of Chart.yaml, values files, scripts, and source control. Use least-privilege identities for repository access and separate chart retrieval permissions from cluster deployment permissions. Pin versions when repeatability matters, and inspect changes before promoting a chart artifact.\n\nValidation and troubleshooting: When the operation fails, classify the failure before changing configuration. Check chart metadata and syntax, repository URL and DNS resolution, TLS trust, authentication, version availability, lock file consistency, package retrieval, and rendered output in a deliberate order. Preserve error messages and pipeline logs. Change one variable at a time, repeat the validation, and document the confirmed root cause rather than hiding the failure with an unrelated workaround.\n\nRelease readiness: Before production use, verify the chart name, chart version, source repository or registry, dependency versions, values supplied, and generated resource changes. For Kubernetes resources, review namespaces, labels, selectors, service accounts, security contexts, resource requests and limits, storage settings, and exposed service endpoints where relevant. Test in a controlled namespace, retain the exact artifact that passed validation, and promote that reviewed artifact through the release process."
      },
