        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 8, section 9: Repository Trust and Supply Chain Security. Explain how organizations evaluate chart publishers, source trust, package integrity, provenance information, and signing or verification controls supported by their Helm workflow. Describe why charts must be reviewed as executable infrastructure definitions.\n\nTechnical workflow and implementation: Start by inspecting the chart directory, the installed Helm version, and the relevant chart metadata. Identify the input files, repository configuration, dependency declarations, and values that control the operation. Use the appropriate Helm commands to validate the configuration and inspect the result. For chart authoring tasks, run helm lint and helm template with the intended values files. For repository or dependency tasks, verify the source, resolved version, downloaded artifact, and package contents. Do not assume that a successful command proves the resulting chart is suitable for production.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Create a small test chart or use an approved sample chart in a non-production workspace. Apply the configuration described in this section, execute the relevant Helm workflow, and capture the command output. Inspect the resulting metadata, dependency package, or rendered Kubernetes manifests. Repeat the procedure from a clean workspace or CI runner to confirm that the result does not depend on undocumented local settings. Record the exact chart version and configuration used during the exercise.\n\nProduction engineering considerations: Explain how organizations evaluate chart publishers, source trust, package integrity, provenance information, and signing or verification controls supported by their Helm workflow. Describe why charts must be reviewed as executable infrastructure definitions. Apply peer review to changes affecting chart sources, dependency versions, repository configuration, credentials, and release values. Keep secrets out of Chart