  {
    "topicTitle": "Release Installation",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-9-section-01",
        "title": "Helm Release Installation Fundamentals",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 9, section 1: Helm Release Installation Fundamentals. Explain the meaning of a Helm release and how helm install creates a named deployment instance from a chart. Cover the relationship between chart package, release name, namespace, supplied values, rendered templates, and Kubernetes resources. Distinguish installing a chart from simply downloading or rendering it.\n\nTechnical explanation: Helm manages a release by combining chart metadata, templates, values, and Kubernetes API operations. Engineers must understand which inputs determine the generated manifests and which cluster resources are affected. Inspect the chart version, release name, namespace, supplied values, and current release state before changing anything. Separate chart-level behavior from Kubernetes controller behavior so that a Helm command result is not mistaken for proof of application health.\n\nPractical implementation: Use a dedicated test namespace and an approved sample chart to reproduce the workflow. Inspect Chart.yaml and values.yaml, run helm lint, and render the expected manifests with helm template. Execute the relevant release command only after reviewing the generated resources. Verify the resulting state using helm status, helm history, kubectl get, kubectl describe, and application logs where appropriate. Record the exact commands, chart version, values files, and release revision.\n\nProduction DevOps practice: Explain the meaning of a Helm release and how helm install creates a named deployment instance from a chart. Cover the relationship between chart package, release name, namespace, supplied values, rendered templates, and Kubernetes resources. Distinguish installing a chart from simply downloading or rendering it. Use explicit chart versions and reviewed configuration. Protect cluster credentials and use a deployment identity with only the permissions required for the target namespace. Require peer review for changes that affect workload images, resource limits, service exposure, persistent storage, security contexts, or access permissions. Keep release artifacts and deployment records traceable to the source commit and pipeline execution.\n\nTroubleshooting approach: When a release operation fails, preserve the Helm error and inspect the release status before attempting another operation. Review Kubernetes events, pod scheduling, image pull status, container logs, readiness and liveness probes, storage attachment, and API admission errors. For upgrade problems, compare the intended manifests with the current release revision. For rollback problems, confirm the selected revision and check whether external data or application state can safely support the restored version.\n\nVerification and operational outcome: Define success using observable conditions rather than the command exit code alone. Confirm that the expected revision is active, workloads reach the required readiness state, services expose the correct endpoints, and application health checks pass. Check that resource requests, limits, security settings, and environment-specific configuration remain correct. Document the outcome and retain the exact chart package and values used so another engineer can reproduce or investigate the release."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-9-section-02",
        "title": "Choosing Release Names and Namespaces",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 9, section 2: Choosing Release Names and Namespaces. Explain how release names identify deployed chart instances and how Kubernetes namespaces provide resource organization and isolation. Cover naming conventions, namespace creation behavior, release uniqueness, multi-team environments, and the operational consequences of installing into an unintended namespace.\n\nTechnical explanation: Helm manages a release by combining chart metadata, templates, values, and Kubernetes API operations. Engineers must understand which inputs determine the generated manifests and which cluster resources are affected. Inspect the chart version, release name, namespace, supplied values, and current release state before changing anything. Separate chart-level behavior from Kubernetes controller behavior so that a Helm command result is not mistaken for proof of application health.\n\nPractical implementation: Use a dedicated test namespace and an approved sample chart to reproduce the workflow. Inspect Chart.yaml and values.yaml, run helm lint, and render the expected manifests with helm template. Execute the relevant release command only after reviewing the generated resources. Verify the resulting state using helm status, helm history, kubectl get, kubectl describe, and application logs where appropriate. Record the exact commands, chart version, values files, and release revision.\n\nProduction DevOps practice: Explain how release names identify deployed chart instances and how Kubernetes namespaces provide resource organization and isolation. Cover naming conventions, namespace creation behavior, release uniqueness, multi-team environments, and the operational consequences of installing into an unintended namespace. Use explicit chart versions and reviewed configuration. Protect cluster credentials and use a deployment identity with only the permissions required for the target namespace. Require peer review for changes that affect workload images, resource limits, service exposure, persistent storage, security contexts, or access permissions. Keep release artifacts and deployment records traceable to the source commit and pipeline execution.\n\nTroubleshooting approach: When a release operation fails, preserve the Helm error and inspect the release status before attempting another operation. Review Kubernetes events, pod scheduling, image pull status, container logs, readiness and liveness probes, storage attachment, and API admission errors. For upgrade problems, compare the intended manifests with the current release revision. For rollback problems, confirm the selected revision and check whether external data or application state can safely support the restored version.\n\nVerification and operational outcome: Define success using observable conditions rather than the command exit code alone. Confirm that the expected revision is active, workloads reach the required readiness state, services expose the correct endpoints, and application health checks pass. Check that resource requests, limits, security settings, and environment-specific configuration remain correct. Document the outcome and retain the exact chart package and values used so another engineer can reproduce or investigate the release."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-9-section-03",
        "title": "Installing a Chart with Explicit Versions",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 9, section 3: Installing a Chart with Explicit Versions. Explain how to install a chart using a specific chart version and why production deployments should avoid relying on an unspecified latest version. Distinguish chart version from application version and describe how repository selection and version pinning improve traceability.\n\nTechnical explanation: Helm manages a release by combining chart metadata, templates, values, and Kubernetes API operations. Engineers must understand which inputs determine the generated manifests and which cluster resources are affected. Inspect the chart version, release name, namespace, supplied values, and current release state before changing anything. Separate chart-level behavior from Kubernetes controller behavior so that a Helm command result is not mistaken for proof of application health.\n\nPractical implementation: Use a dedicated test namespace and an approved sample chart to reproduce the workflow. Inspect Chart.yaml and values.yaml, run helm lint, and render the expected manifests with helm template. Execute the relevant release command only after reviewing the generated resources. Verify the resulting state using helm status, helm history, kubectl get, kubectl describe, and application logs where appropriate. Record the exact commands, chart version, values files, and release revision.\n\nProduction DevOps practice: Explain how to install a chart using a specific chart version and why production deployments should avoid relying on an unspecified latest version. Distinguish chart version from application version and describe how repository selection and version pinning improve traceability. Use explicit chart versions and reviewed configuration. Protect cluster credentials and use a deployment identity with only the permissions required for the target namespace. Require peer review for changes that affect workload images, resource limits, service exposure, persistent storage, security contexts, or access permissions. Keep release artifacts and deployment records traceable to the source commit and pipeline execution.\n\nTroubleshooting approach: When a release operation fails, preserve the Helm error and inspect the release status before attempting another operation. Review Kubernetes events, pod scheduling, image pull status, container logs, readiness and liveness probes, storage attachment, and API admission errors. For upgrade problems, compare the intended manifests with the current release revision. For rollback problems, confirm the selected revision and check whether external data or application state can safely support the restored version.\n\nVerification and operational outcome: Define success using observable conditions rather than the command exit code alone. Confirm that the expected revision is active, workloads reach the required readiness state, services expose the correct endpoints, and application health checks pass. Check that resource requests, limits, security settings, and environment-specific configuration remain correct. Document the outcome and retain the exact chart package and values used so another engineer can reproduce or investigate the release."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-9-section-04",
        "title": "Supplying Values During Installation",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 9, section 4: Supplying Values During Installation. Explain how Helm receives configuration through values.yaml, additional values files, and command-line overrides. Cover the precedence of values sources, nested configuration, environment-specific settings, and the importance of reviewing effective configuration before deploying.\n\nTechnical explanation: Helm manages a release by combining chart metadata, templates, values, and Kubernetes API operations. Engineers must understand which inputs determine the generated manifests and which cluster resources are affected. Inspect the chart version, release name, namespace, supplied values, and current release state before changing anything. Separate chart-level behavior from Kubernetes controller behavior so that a Helm command result is not mistaken for proof of application health.\n\nPractical implementation: Use a dedicated test namespace and an approved sample chart to reproduce the workflow. Inspect Chart.yaml and values.yaml, run helm lint, and render the expected manifests with helm template. Execute the relevant release command only after reviewing the generated resources. Verify the resulting state using helm status, helm history, kubectl get, kubectl describe, and application logs where appropriate. Record the exact commands, chart version, values files, and release revision.\n\nProduction DevOps practice: Explain how Helm receives configuration through values.yaml, additional values files, and command-line overrides. Cover the precedence of values sources, nested configuration, environment-specific settings, and the importance of reviewing effective configuration before deploying. Use explicit chart versions and reviewed configuration. Protect cluster credentials and use a deployment identity with only the permissions required for the target namespace. Require peer review for changes that affect workload images, resource limits, service exposure, persistent storage, security contexts, or access permissions. Keep release artifacts and deployment records traceable to the source commit and pipeline execution.\n\nTroubleshooting approach: When a release operation fails, preserve the Helm error and inspect the release status before attempting another operation. Review Kubernetes events, pod scheduling, image pull status, container logs, readiness and liveness probes, storage attachment, and API admission errors. For upgrade problems, compare the intended manifests with the current release revision. For rollback problems, confirm the selected revision and check whether external data or application state can safely support the restored version.\n\nVerification and operational outcome: Define success using observable conditions rather than the command exit code alone. Confirm that the expected revision is active, workloads reach the required readiness state, services expose the correct endpoints, and application health checks pass. Check that resource requests, limits, security settings, and environment-specific configuration remain correct. Document the outcome and retain the exact chart package and values used so another engineer can reproduce or investigate the release."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-9-section-05",
        "title": "Helm Template Rendering Before Installation",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 9, section 5: Helm Template Rendering Before Installation. Explain how Helm converts chart templates and values into Kubernetes manifests before resources are submitted to the cluster. Describe helm template, template evaluation, generated resource inspection, and how pre-install rendering helps detect invalid names, missing values, and unexpected configuration.\n\nTechnical explanation: Helm manages a release by combining chart metadata, templates, values, and Kubernetes API operations. Engineers must understand which inputs determine the generated manifests and which cluster resources are affected. Inspect the chart version, release name, namespace, supplied values, and current release state before changing anything. Separate chart-level behavior from Kubernetes controller behavior so that a Helm command result is not mistaken for proof of application health.\n\nPractical implementation: Use a dedicated test namespace and an approved sample chart to reproduce the workflow. Inspect Chart.yaml and values.yaml, run helm lint, and render the expected manifests with helm template. Execute the relevant release command only after reviewing the generated resources. Verify the resulting state using helm status, helm history, kubectl get, kubectl describe, and application logs where appropriate. Record the exact commands, chart version, values files, and release revision.\n\nProduction DevOps practice: Explain how Helm converts chart templates and values into Kubernetes manifests before resources are submitted to the cluster. Describe helm template, template evaluation, generated resource inspection, and how pre-install rendering helps detect invalid names, missing values, and unexpected configuration. Use explicit chart versions and reviewed configuration. Protect cluster credentials and use a deployment identity with only the permissions required for the target namespace. Require peer review for changes that affect workload images, resource limits, service exposure, persistent storage, security contexts, or access permissions. Keep release artifacts and deployment records traceable to the source commit and pipeline execution.\n\nTroubleshooting approach: When a release operation fails, preserve the Helm error and inspect the release status before attempting another operation. Review Kubernetes events, pod scheduling, image pull status, container logs, readiness and liveness probes, storage attachment, and API admission errors. For upgrade problems, compare the intended manifests with the current release revision. For rollback problems, confirm the selected revision and check whether external data or application state can safely support the restored version.\n\nVerification and operational outcome: Define success using observable conditions rather than the command exit code alone. Confirm that the expected revision is active, workloads reach the required readiness state, services expose the correct endpoints, and application health checks pass. Check that resource requests, limits, security settings, and environment-specific configuration remain correct. Document the outcome and retain the exact chart package and values used so another engineer can reproduce or investigate the release."
