  {
    "topicTitle": "Environment-specific Values",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-11-section-01",
        "title": "Environment-specific Values Fundamentals",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 11, section 1: Environment-specific Values Fundamentals. Explain why the same Helm chart is configured differently across development, testing, staging, and production. Cover the separation between reusable chart templates and environment-dependent configuration such as replica counts, image references, service exposure, resource sizing, storage, and feature flags. Explain how a consistent chart reduces configuration drift.\n\nTechnical implementation and workflow: Begin by identifying the chart, configuration source, cluster, namespace, and release or application involved. Inspect the relevant metadata and configuration before changing it. For Helm workflows, use helm lint and helm template with the exact values files intended for the target environment. For GitOps workflows, inspect the repository revision, Application source and destination, generated manifests, and current synchronization state. Keep the desired configuration reviewable and reproducible.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Build a small test application with a clear separation between shared configuration and environment-specific settings. Render or deploy it in a non-production namespace and record the resulting manifests. Where Argo CD is involved, connect a test repository, create an Application definition, and observe how the controller evaluates the desired state. Repeat the workflow from a clean environment and verify that no undocumented local configuration is required.\n\nProduction engineering considerations: Explain why the same Helm chart is configured differently across development, testing, staging, and production. Cover the separation between reusable chart templates and environment-dependent configuration such as replica counts, image references, service exposure, resource sizing, storage, and feature flags. Explain how a consistent chart reduces configuration drift. Use peer review for changes that affect environment configuration, image versions, resource allocation, service exposure, repository credentials, cluster destinations, and application permissions. Keep sensitive information outside ordinary source-controlled values. Use protected CI variables or an approved external secret system. Apply least privilege to deployment identities and restrict which repositories and destinations an application can use.\n\nTroubleshooting and validation: When the expected state is not reached, preserve the error output and identify the failing layer. Check configuration syntax, missing values, version selection, repository access, manifest generation, Kubernetes API acceptance, resource scheduling, readiness, and application health. For GitOps reconciliation, compare the committed revision with the observed live state and inspect controller or repository server logs. Avoid manual live edits that create additional drift without a documented recovery plan.\n\nOperational readiness: Define the expected result before deployment. Verify the selected environment, chart or manifest revision, destination cluster, namespace, generated resource changes, workload readiness, and application health. Review security settings, service exposure, resource requests and limits, persistence, and access boundaries. Retain the source revision and deployment evidence so the release can be audited, reproduced, and safely recovered."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-11-section-02",
        "title": "Designing Environment Values Files",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 11, section 2: Designing Environment Values Files. Explain how teams organize values-dev.yaml, values-test.yaml, values-staging.yaml, and values-prod.yaml. Describe what belongs in a shared values.yaml file and what belongs in an environment override. Cover naming conventions, review ownership, configuration boundaries, and how to avoid copying entire configuration files unnecessarily.\n\nTechnical implementation and workflow: Begin by identifying the chart, configuration source, cluster, namespace, and release or application involved. Inspect the relevant metadata and configuration before changing it. For Helm workflows, use helm lint and helm template with the exact values files intended for the target environment. For GitOps workflows, inspect the repository revision, Application source and destination, generated manifests, and current synchronization state. Keep the desired configuration reviewable and reproducible.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Build a small test application with a clear separation between shared configuration and environment-specific settings. Render or deploy it in a non-production namespace and record the resulting manifests. Where Argo CD is involved, connect a test repository, create an Application definition, and observe how the controller evaluates the desired state. Repeat the workflow from a clean environment and verify that no undocumented local configuration is required.\n\nProduction engineering considerations: Explain how teams organize values-dev.yaml, values-test.yaml, values-staging.yaml, and values-prod.yaml. Describe what belongs in a shared values.yaml file and what belongs in an environment override. Cover naming conventions, review ownership, configuration boundaries, and how to avoid copying entire configuration files unnecessarily. Use peer review for changes that affect environment configuration, image versions, resource allocation, service exposure, repository credentials, cluster destinations, and application permissions. Keep sensitive information outside ordinary source-controlled values. Use protected CI variables or an approved external secret system. Apply least privilege to deployment identities and restrict which repositories and destinations an application can use.\n\nTroubleshooting and validation: When the expected state is not reached, preserve the error output and identify the failing layer. Check configuration syntax, missing values, version selection, repository access, manifest generation, Kubernetes API acceptance, resource scheduling, readiness, and application health. For GitOps reconciliation, compare the committed revision with the observed live state and inspect controller or repository server logs. Avoid manual live edits that create additional drift without a documented recovery plan.\n\nOperational readiness: Define the expected result before deployment. Verify the selected environment, chart or manifest revision, destination cluster, namespace, generated resource changes, workload readiness, and application health. Review security settings, service exposure, resource requests and limits, persistence, and access boundaries. Retain the source revision and deployment evidence so the release can be audited, reproduced, and safely recovered."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-11-section-03",
        "title": "Helm Values Precedence and Overrides",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 11, section 3: Helm Values Precedence and Overrides. Explain how Helm combines chart defaults, parent chart values, additional values files, and command-line overrides. Describe how later overrides can replace earlier settings and how nested maps and lists require careful review. Explain why operators should understand the effective configuration instead of assuming one file contains every final value.\n\nTechnical implementation and workflow: Begin by identifying the chart, configuration source, cluster, namespace, and release or application involved. Inspect the relevant metadata and configuration before changing it. For Helm workflows, use helm lint and helm template with the exact values files intended for the target environment. For GitOps workflows, inspect the repository revision, Application source and destination, generated manifests, and current synchronization state. Keep the desired configuration reviewable and reproducible.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Build a small test application with a clear separation between shared configuration and environment-specific settings. Render or deploy it in a non-production namespace and record the resulting manifests. Where Argo CD is involved, connect a test repository, create an Application definition, and observe how the controller evaluates the desired state. Repeat the workflow from a clean environment and verify that no undocumented local configuration is required.\n\nProduction engineering considerations: Explain how Helm combines chart defaults, parent chart values, additional values files, and command-line overrides. Describe how later overrides can replace earlier settings and how nested maps and lists require careful review. Explain why operators should understand the effective configuration instead of assuming one file contains every final value. Use peer review for changes that affect environment configuration, image versions, resource allocation, service exposure, repository credentials, cluster destinations, and application permissions. Keep sensitive information outside ordinary source-controlled values. Use protected CI variables or an approved external secret system. Apply least privilege to deployment identities and restrict which repositories and destinations an application can use.\n\nTroubleshooting and validation: When the expected state is not reached, preserve the error output and identify the failing layer. Check configuration syntax, missing values, version selection, repository access, manifest generation, Kubernetes API acceptance, resource scheduling, readiness, and application health. For GitOps reconciliation, compare the committed revision with the observed live state and inspect controller or repository server logs. Avoid manual live edits that create additional drift without a documented recovery plan.\n\nOperational readiness: Define the expected result before deployment. Verify the selected environment, chart or manifest revision, destination cluster, namespace, generated resource changes, workload readiness, and application health. Review security settings, service exposure, resource requests and limits, persistence, and access boundaries. Retain the source revision and deployment evidence so the release can be audited, reproduced, and safely recovered."
