tion. Keep application ownership explicit, restrict trusted source repositories and deployment destinations, and review changes that affect deletion, synchronization, cluster access, or production resource ownership. Document the expected recovery procedure before introducing a high-impact configuration change.\n\nTroubleshooting and validation: Start with the Application conditions and identify whether the failure occurs during authentication, repository retrieval, manifest generation, state comparison, synchronization, or Kubernetes resource health evaluation. Inspect the responsible component logs and Kubernetes events. Confirm the selected repository revision, source path, destination cluster, namespace, project policy, and resource permissions. Correct the root cause and allow Argo CD to reconcile the reviewed desired state.\n\nOperational readiness and evidence: Before considering the task complete, verify the Application definition, source revision, generated manifests, destination, managed resource ownership, synchronization result, and health of deployed workloads. Check that no unrelated resources were modified or deleted. Record the configuration change, approval, relevant logs, and validation results so another engineer can understand the deployment and safely repeat or recover the operation."
