        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 13, section 3: Application Controller Internals. Explain how the application controller continuously observes Application resources, requests desired manifests, compares them with live Kubernetes objects, and records application status. Cover reconciliation queues, resource tracking, sync operations, health evaluation, and the importance of controller capacity when many applications and resources are managed.\n\nArchitecture and implementation details: Identify the Argo CD component, Kubernetes resource, API interaction, configuration field, and operational dependency involved in this topic. Trace the flow from the declared configuration to the controller or service responsible for processing it. Inspect the actual Application specification, component configuration, generated manifests, and live Kubernetes objects instead of relying only on a user-interface summary.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Use a non-production cluster and a version-controlled test repository to reproduce the workflow. Define the required source and destination settings, apply the configuration through a reviewed change, and observe how Argo CD processes it. Capture the relevant Application status, generated resources, controller events, and component logs. Repeat the exercise after introducing one controlled configuration error and identify the exact layer that reports it.\n\nProduction engineering and security: Explain how the application controller continuously observes Application resources, requests desired manifests, compares them with live Kubernetes objects, and records application status. Cover reconciliation queues, resource tracking, sync operations, health evaluation, and the importance of controller capacity when many applications and resources are managed. Apply least-privilege permissions to users, service accounts, repositories, and destination clusters. Protect credentials and use TLS for service communication. Keep application ownership 