        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 14, section 2: Application Metadata and Naming. Explain Application metadata including name, namespace, labels, annotations, and owner references where applicable. Cover naming conventions that identify teams, services, environments, and ownership. Explain how labels support filtering and automation while annotations can carry operational metadata, and why metadata should remain consistent and should not contain credentials.\n\nArchitecture and implementation details: Identify the Argo CD component, Kubernetes resource, API interaction, configuration field, and operational dependency involved in this topic. Trace the flow from the declared configuration to the controller or service responsible for processing it. Inspect the actual Application specification, component configuration, generated manifests, and live Kubernetes objects instead of relying only on a user-interface summary.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Use a non-production cluster and a version-controlled test repository to reproduce the workflow. Define the required source and destination settings, apply the configuration through a reviewed change, and observe how Argo CD processes it. Capture the relevant Application status, generated resources, controller events, and component logs. Repeat the exercise after introducing one controlled configuration error and identify the exact layer that reports it.\n\nProduction engineering and security: Explain Application metadata including name, namespace, labels, annotations, and owner references where applicable. Cover naming conventions that identify teams, services, environments, and ownership. Explain how labels support filtering and automation while annotations can carry operational metadata, and why metadata should remain consistent and should not contain credentials. Apply least-privilege permissions to users, service accounts, repositories, and destination clusters. Protect credentials and use TLS for service communication. Keep application ownership explicit, restrict trusted source repositories and deployment destinations, and review changes that affect deletion, synchronization, cluster access, or production resource ownership. Document the expected recovery procedure before introducing a high-impact configuration change.\n\nTroubleshooting and validation: Start with the Application conditions and identify whether the failure occurs during authentication, repository retrieval, manifest generation, state comparison, synchronization, or Kubernetes resource health evaluation. Inspect the responsible component logs and Kubernetes events. Confirm the selected repository revision, source path, destination cluster, namespace, project policy, and resource permissions. Correct the root cause and allow Argo CD to reconcile the reviewed desired state.\n\nOperational readiness and evidence: Before considering the task complete, verify the Application definition, source revision, generated manifests, destination, managed resource ownership, synchronization result, and health of deployed workloads. Check that no unrelated resources were modified or deleted. Record the configuration change, approval, relevant logs, and validation results so another engineer can understand the deployment and safely repeat or recover the operation."
