  {
    "topicTitle": "GitOps Workflow",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-15-section-01",
        "title": "GitOps Workflow Fundamentals",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 15, section 1: GitOps Workflow Fundamentals. Explain GitOps as an operational model in which declarative application and infrastructure configuration is maintained in version control and continuously reconciled with the target environment. Describe the roles of developers, reviewers, Git repositories, CI pipelines, Argo CD, and Kubernetes. Explain how Git provides an auditable record of desired state and why deployment outcomes must be verified in the cluster.\n\nWorkflow and implementation details: Identify the Git repository, selected revision, Argo CD Application, source configuration, destination cluster, namespace, and synchronization policy involved. Trace the desired configuration from the reviewed source change to manifest generation, comparison, and the resulting Kubernetes resources. Inspect the Application specification and actual generated output rather than assuming that a successful Git commit guarantees a successful deployment.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Use a disposable namespace and a non-production application to reproduce the workflow. Make one controlled change in Git, observe how Argo CD refreshes and compares the desired state, and record the sync result. Where automated sync or self-healing applies, introduce a safe test change and observe the controller response. Verify the final resource configuration and workload health before repeating the exercise with another controlled scenario.\n\nProduction engineering and security: Explain GitOps as an operational model in which declarative application and infrastructure configuration is maintained in version control and continuously reconciled with the target environment. Describe the roles of developers, reviewers, Git repositories, CI pipelines, Argo CD, and Kubernetes. Explain how Git provides an auditable record of desired state and why deployment outcomes must be verified in the cluster. Protect the source repository with branch controls, peer review, required validation, and appropriate access restrictions. Use immutable image references and keep sensitive credentials in an approved secret-management workflow. Review destructive operations, resource ownership, destination permissions, and deployment windows. Ensure operators understand the consequences of automated reconciliation before enabling it for production workloads.\n\nTroubleshooting and recovery: When the observed result differs from expectations, determine whether the problem is in Git revision selection, repository access, manifest generation, comparison, sync policy, project restrictions, Kubernetes authorization, or workload health. Inspect Application conditions, operation details, controller logs, repository server logs, and Kubernetes events. Correct the authoritative configuration through a reviewed source change and verify that reconciliation reaches the intended state.\n\nOperational readiness and evidence: Confirm the exact repository revision, generated manifests, target cluster and namespace, synchronization policy, managed resource set, sync result, and workload health. Check whether pruning or self-healing could affect stateful or shared resources. Retain the relevant commit, pull request, operation history, logs, and validation evidence so the deployment can be audited, reproduced, and safely recovered."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-15-section-02",
        "title": "Git Repository Structure for GitOps",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 15, section 2: Git Repository Structure for GitOps. Explain how teams organize application source code, Helm charts, Kustomize overlays, shared platform configuration, and environment-specific manifests in Git repositories. Compare application repositories with dedicated deployment configuration repositories. Cover directory conventions, ownership boundaries, repository permissions, and how a clear structure makes changes discoverable and reduces accidental cross-environment modifications.\n\nWorkflow and implementation details: Identify the Git repository, selected revision, Argo CD Application, source configuration, destination cluster, namespace, and synchronization policy involved. Trace the desired configuration from the reviewed source change to manifest generation, comparison, and the resulting Kubernetes resources. Inspect the Application specification and actual generated output rather than assuming that a successful Git commit guarantees a successful deployment.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Use a disposable namespace and a non-production application to reproduce the workflow. Make one controlled change in Git, observe how Argo CD refreshes and compares the desired state, and record the sync result. Where automated sync or self-healing applies, introduce a safe test change and observe the controller response. Verify the final resource configuration and workload health before repeating the exercise with another controlled scenario.\n\nProduction engineering and security: Explain how teams organize application source code, Helm charts, Kustomize overlays, shared platform configuration, and environment-specific manifests in Git repositories. Compare application repositories with dedicated deployment configuration repositories. Cover directory conventions, ownership boundaries, repository permissions, and how a clear structure makes changes discoverable and reduces accidental cross-environment modifications. Protect the source repository with branch controls, peer review, required validation, and appropriate access restrictions. Use immutable image references and keep sensitive credentials in an approved secret-management workflow. Review destructive operations, resource ownership, destination permissions, and deployment windows. Ensure operators understand the consequences of automated reconciliation before enabling it for production workloads.\n\nTroubleshooting and recovery: When the observed result differs from expectations, determine whether the problem is in Git revision selection, repository access, manifest generation, comparison, sync policy, project restrictions, Kubernetes authorization, or workload health. Inspect Application conditions, operation details, controller logs, repository server logs, and Kubernetes events. Correct the authoritative configuration through a reviewed source change and verify that reconciliation reaches the intended state.\n\nOperational readiness and evidence: Confirm the exact repository revision, generated manifests, target cluster and namespace, synchronization policy, managed resource set, sync result, and workload health. Check whether pruning or self-healing could affect stateful or shared resources. Retain the relevant commit, pull request, operation history, logs, and validation evidence so the deployment can be audited, reproduced, and safely recovered."
