        "id": "stage-11-topic-16-section-14",
        "title": "Troubleshooting Automated Sync and Self-healing",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 16, section 14: Troubleshooting Automated Sync and Self-healing. Explain how to troubleshoot an application that detects changes but does not synchronize, repeatedly becomes OutOfSync, or fails to restore manually modified resources. Cover sync policy configuration, project restrictions, sync windows, repository access, manifest generation, destination permissions, resource ownership, controller logs, and Kubernetes events. Explain how to isolate policy restrictions from technical failures.\n\nWorkflow and implementation details: Identify the Git repository, selected revision, Argo CD Application, source configuration, destination cluster, namespace, and synchronization policy involved. Trace the desired configuration from the reviewed source change to manifest generation, comparison, and the resulting Kubernetes resources. Inspect the Application specification and actual generated output rather than assuming that a successful Git commit guarantees a successful deployment.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Use a disposable namespace and a non-production application to reproduce the workflow. Make one controlled change in Git, observe how Argo CD refreshes and compares the desired state, and record the sync result. Where automated sync or self-healing applies, introduce a safe test change and observe the controller response. Verify the final resource configuration and workload health before repeating the exercise with another controlled scenario.\n\nProduction engineering and security: Explain how to troubleshoot an application that detects changes but does not synchronize, repeatedly becomes OutOfSync, or fails to restore manually modified resources. Cover sync policy configuration, project restrictions, sync windows, repository access, manifest generation, destination permissions, resource ownership, controller logs, and Kubernetes events. Explain how to isolate policy restrictions from technical failures. Protect the source repository with branch controls, peer review, required validation, and appropriate access restrictions. Use immutable image references and keep sensitive credentials in an approved secret-management workflow. Review destructive operations, resource ownership, destination permissions, and deployment windows. Ensure operators understand the consequences of automated reconciliation before enabling it for production workloads.\n\nTroubleshooting and recovery: When the observed result differs from expectations, determine whether the problem is in Git revision selection, repository access, manifest generation, comparison, sync policy, project restrictions, Kubernetes authorization, or workload health. Inspect Application conditions, operation details, controller logs, repository server logs, and Kubernetes events. Correct the authoritative configuration through a reviewed source change and verify that reconciliation reaches the intended state.\n\nOperational readiness and evidence: Confirm the exact repository revision, generated manifests, target cluster and namespace, synchronization policy, managed resource set, sync result, and workload health. Check whether pruning or self-healing could affect stateful or shared resources. Retain the relevant commit, pull request, operation history, logs, and validation evidence so the deployment can be audited, reproduced, and safely recovered."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-16-section-15",
        "title": "Production Self-healing Recovery Lab",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 16, section 15: Production Self-healing Recovery Lab. Describe a controlled lab in which an application is deployed through Argo CD, automated sync and self-healing are configured, and a managed Deployment is deliberately modified outside Git. Explain how to observe drift, identify the responsible Application, confirm reconciliation, and verify that the expected replica count and container configuration are restored. Include safety boundaries, evidence collection, and post-exercise review.\n\nWorkflow and implementation details: Identify the Git repository, selected revision, Argo CD Application, source configuration, destination cluster, namespace, and synchronization policy involved. Trace the desired configuration from the reviewed source change to manifest generation, comparison, and the resulting Kubernetes resources. Inspect the Application specification and actual generated output rather than assuming that a successful Git commit guarantees a successful deployment.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Use a disposable namespace and a non-production application to reproduce the workflow. Make one controlled change in Git, observe how Argo CD refreshes and compares the desired state, and record the sync result. Where automated sync or self-healing applies, introduce a safe test change and observe the controller response. Verify the final resource configuration and workload health before repeating the exercise with another controlled scenario.\n\nProduction engineering and security: Describe a controlled lab in which an application is deployed through Argo CD, automated sync and self-healing are configured, and a managed Deployment is deliberately modified outside Git. Explain how to observe drift, identify the responsible Application, confirm reconciliation, and verify that the expected replica count and container configuration are restored. Include safety boundaries, evidence collection, and post-exercise review. Protect the source repository with branch controls, peer review, required validation, and appropriate access restrictions. Use immutable image references and keep sensitive credentials in an approved secret-management workflow. Review destructive operations, resource ownership, destination permissions, and deployment windows. Ensure operators understand the consequences of automated reconciliation before enabling it for production workloads.\n\nTroubleshooting and recovery: When the observed result differs from expectations, determine whether the problem is in Git revision selection, repository access, manifest generation, comparison, sync policy, project restrictions, Kubernetes authorization, or workload health. Inspect Application conditions, operation details, controller logs, repository server logs, and Kubernetes events. Correct the authoritative configuration through a reviewed source change and verify that reconciliation reaches the intended state.\n\nOperational readiness and evidence: Confirm the exact repository revision, generated manifests, target cluster and namespace, synchronization policy, managed resource set, sync result, and workload health. Check whether pruning or self-healing could affect stateful or shared resources. Retain the relevant commit, pull request, operation history, logs, and validation evidence so the deployment can be audited, reproduced, and safely recovered."
      }
