        "title": "Drift in Helm and Kustomize Applications",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 18, section 10: Drift in Helm and Kustomize Applications. Explain how changes to Helm values, chart versions, templates, Kustomize overlays, and patches can change the generated desired state and create drift against live resources. Cover the importance of consistent rendering inputs and source revisions. Explain how to inspect generated manifests and determine whether a difference comes from Git configuration, rendering behavior, or an external live change.\n\nTechnical workflow and evidence: Identify the Argo CD Application, source repository, target revision, destination cluster, namespace, and Kubernetes resources involved. Inspect the Application status and resource tree, then compare the desired manifests with the live objects. Capture the relevant resource diff, conditions, operation information, Kubernetes events, and component logs. Establish whether the observed state is caused by a source change, controller behavior, external modification, or workload failure.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Use a disposable test namespace and a non-production Application. Record the initial desired and live configuration, introduce one controlled change, and observe the resulting status and resource diff. Test the relevant recovery or reconciliation behavior only after confirming resource ownership and safety. Verify the final resource specification, readiness, and application behavior, and retain evidence of the change and its resolution.\n\nProduction engineering and safety: Explain how changes to Helm values, chart versions, templates, Kustomize overlays, and patches can change the generated desired state and create drift against live resources. Cover the importance of consistent rendering inputs and source revisions. Explain how to inspect generated manifests and determine whether a difference comes from Git configuration, rendering behavior, or an external live change. Apply controlled access to live cluster resources and make Git changes through reviewed pull requests. Use narrowly scoped exceptions when fields are intentionally managed by another controller. Before enabling automated correction or pruning, evaluate the impact on stateful workloads, shared resources, and external integrations. Preserve an emergency-change process that records the action and restores agreement between the live system and the approved desired state.\n\nTroubleshooting and validation: Determine whether the issue originates in desired manifests, live object changes, rendering differences, API defaulting, external controllers, resource health, or reconciliation policy. Inspect the exact affected fields and managed ownership before modifying configuration. Validate any proposed correction through manifest rendering and review, then confirm that the comparison result changes as expected and that Kubernetes resources reach the intended operational condition.\n\nOperational readiness: Verify the selected source revision, destination, resource ownership, comparison result, reconciliation outcome, and application health. Confirm that no unrelated resources were changed or removed. Review alerts, audit records, Git history, and relevant logs. Document the root cause, corrective action, approved exceptions, and prevention measures so future operators can distinguish expected differences from a genuine production configuration problem."
      },
