        "id": "stage-11-topic-19-section-09",
        "title": "HTTPS Authentication and Access Tokens",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 19, section 9: HTTPS Authentication and Access Tokens. Explain how HTTPS-based repository authentication works using approved usernames, tokens, or other supported credential mechanisms. Cover token scopes, repository read permissions, expiration, secret storage, and secure credential injection. Explain why tokens should not be embedded in repository URLs, committed to Git, printed in pipeline logs, or reused across unrelated systems.\n\nTechnical investigation and implementation: Identify the affected Application, repository revision, source path, destination cluster, namespace, resource, and responsible Argo CD component. Capture the exact error message and relevant timestamps. Inspect Application conditions, operation details, generated manifests, Kubernetes events, and component logs. Reproduce the issue in a controlled environment when possible and avoid making unverified changes to production resources.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Reproduce the scenario using a non-production application and disposable namespace. Introduce one controlled fault related to the topic, observe the reported symptoms, and collect evidence from Argo CD and Kubernetes. Identify the responsible configuration or component, apply a minimal corrective change through the approved workflow, and verify that synchronization and workload health return to the expected state.\n\nProduction engineering and security: Explain how HTTPS-based repository authentication works using approved usernames, tokens, or other supported credential mechanisms. Cover token scopes, repository read permissions, expiration, secret storage, and secure credential injection. Explain why tokens should not be embedded in repository URLs, committed to Git, printed in pipeline logs, or reused across unrelated systems. Preserve least-privilege access, protect repository and cluster credentials, and follow change approval requirements for production modifications. Avoid exposing tokens, private keys, passwords, or sensitive manifest data in logs and incident reports. Before performing deletion, rollback, credential rotation, or permission changes, understand the potential impact on dependent applications and stateful resources.\n\nRoot-cause validation and recovery: Separate the immediate symptom from the underlying cause. Confirm whether the failure is related to identity, source access, rendering, comparison, policy, destination authorization, resource admission, scheduling, or application health. Correct the authoritative configuration and verify the resulting Git revision, generated manifests, sync operation, resource state, and application health. Do not rely solely on a green status indicator when critical workloads remain unhealthy.\n\nOperational evidence and prevention: Record the incident timeline, affected resources, relevant logs, repository revision, corrective change, approval, and post-recovery checks. Identify whether monitoring, validation, documentation, access controls, or deployment safeguards could prevent recurrence. Update the runbook with verified diagnostic commands and recovery steps so the next engineer can investigate the same failure consistently and safely."
